        <p class="profile-detail-region">Battle · South &amp; Southeast Asia</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of Dien Bien Phu</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">13 March – 7 May 1954</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">The valley of Điện Biên Phủ, deep in the autonomous Tai Federation in northwest Tonkin, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, near the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-union">French Union</a> forces under Colonel Christian de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castries">Castries</a>, with 10,800 troops and reinforcements bringing the total to nearly 16,000; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> forces under General Võ Nguyên Giáp, with up to 50,000 regular troops in five divisions</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The garrison was overrun after a two-month siege and the survivors surrendered. French forces later withdrew from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">Indochina</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva-accords-1954">1954 Geneva Accords</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> was temporarily divided at the 17th parallel.</span></div>
        </div>
