    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The French built an isolated camp supplied by air in a valley whose surrounding high ground the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> controlled, and the Viet Minh brought in artillery and anti-aircraft guns that the French had not expected.</li><li>Casualty figures differ widely: the Vietnamese government reported 4,020 dead, 9,118 wounded and 792 missing, the French estimated 8,000 dead and 15,000 wounded, and Mark Moyar lists 22,900 out of 50,000.</li><li>Of 10,863 prisoners, including Vietnamese fighting for the French, only 3,290 were repatriated four months later; the death rate of French troops in captivity is estimated at about 60%.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Giáp's plan for a quick assault, due to open on 25 January 1954, was cancelled on 26 January after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> intelligence indicated that the French knew of it, and he later called the change of plan the hardest decision of his military career.</p></aside>
    </section>

