    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">May 1953</span><span class="rf-text">Premier René Mayer names Henri Navarre to command <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-union">French Union</a> forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">Indochina</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">November 1953</span><span class="rf-text">Operation Castor begins, and the French drop or fly 9,000 troops into Điện Biên Phủ over three days</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">December 1953</span><span class="rf-text">The garrison of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lai-chau">Lai Chau</a> sets out for Điện Biên Phủ, and only 185 of its 2,100 men arrive by 22 December</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">March 1954</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> assault begins with an attack on the northeastern outpost of Béatrice</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">March 1954</span><span class="rf-text">The Algerian battalion on Gabrielle falls back, abandoning the northern outpost to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">March 1954</span><span class="rf-text">Massed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> assaults open on Dominique and Eliane in the central camp</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">April 1954</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> overrun Huguette 1 and take control of more than 90 percent of the airfield</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">May 1954</span><span class="rf-text">Giáp orders an all-out attack and the remaining French positions are overrun</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-dien-bien-phu" data-v="296e342ec9" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The French fortress and the Viet Minh ring</b> The French held a central headquarters with strong points round it, and Isabelle to the south. The Viet Minh moved divisions and artillery into the hills round the valley and the French were completely surrounded.</li><li><b>Béatrice is overrun</b> The Viet Minh opened with a heavy bombardment of Béatrice, the northeastern outpost. The 312th Division then attacked, and by the early hours of 14 March the position had been taken.</li><li><b>Gabrielle falls</b> The 308th Division attacked Gabrielle from 20:00 on the night of 14 March after a heavy barrage. A French counterattack failed to relieve it, and the Algerian battalion fell back at 08:00 the next day.</li><li><b>Anne-Marie is given up</b> The fall of Béatrice and Gabrielle had demoralised the Tai troops. On the morning of 17 March, under cover of fog, most of them left or defected, and the French and the few Tais who remained withdrew from Anne-Marie.</li><li><b>Dominique 1 and 2 are captured</b> At 19:00 the 312th Division captured Dominique 1 and 2, outflanking all positions east of the river. French howitzers firing at zero elevation and anti-aircraft machine guns near the airfield then forced the attackers to retreat.</li><li><b>Eliane 1 falls and Huguette 7 holds</b> The 316th Division captured Eliane 1 and half of Eliane 2 by midnight. In the west the 308th Division attacked Huguette 7 and nearly broke through, but a French sergeant took charge of the defenders and sealed the breach.</li><li><b>The French counterattack in the east</b> The French retook Eliane 2 just after midnight. On the afternoon of 1 April a counterattack by everybody left who could be trusted to fight retook Dominique 2 and Eliane 1.</li><li><b>The Viet Minh renew the assault</b> The Viet Minh assaulted again and the exhausted French, who had no reserves, fell back from both positions late in the afternoon. A column sent north from Isabelle was attacked on the way and fell back to Isabelle.</li><li><b>Eliane 2 holds and Huguette 7 is retaken</b> The 316th Division attacked Eliane 2 and a few French tanks arrived from the central garrison and helped push it back. The Viet Minh briefly captured Huguette 7, then were pushed back by a French counterattack at dawn on 1 April.</li><li><b>Sapping, and Eliane 1 retaken</b> After heavy losses in the open on 5 April, Giáp turned to entrenchment and sapping. On 10 April the French retook Eliane 1 after it changed hands several times, and a Viet Minh attempt to regain it on 12 April was pushed back.</li><li><b>Huguette 1 is overrun</b> Viet Minh trenches almost surrounded Huguette 1 and 6. French sorties to supply Huguette 6 cost heavy casualties, the defenders of Huguette 6 broke out on 18 April, and Huguette 1 was overrun on 22 April.</li><li><b>Eliane 1, Dominique 3 and Huguette 5 fall</b> The Viet Minh attacked the exhausted defenders in mass on the night of 1 May and overran Eliane 1, Dominique 3 and Huguette 5. The French beat back the attacks on Eliane 2.</li><li><b>Eliane 2 is mined and overrun</b> The Viet Minh attacked Eliane 2 with Katyusha rockets and French artillery broke the first wave. Later that night a mine under Eliane 2 was detonated and within a few hours the defenders were overrun.</li><li><b>The last attack on the headquarters</b> Giáp ordered an all-out attack on the remaining French units. By evening enemy troops stood outside the headquarters bunker and the last radio report said the positions had been overrun.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/first-indochina-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-indochina-war.jpg" alt="First Indochina War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/first-indochina-war">First Indochina War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:First_Indochina_War_COLLAGE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mohamed Ajjani, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By 1953 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-indochina-war">First Indochina War</a> had passed through a succession of French commanders, among them Leclerc, de Lattre de Tassigny and Salan, none of whom had suppressed the insurrection of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a>, who were fighting for independence. In their 1952–1953 campaign the Viet Minh overran much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>, advancing as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luang-prabang">Luang Prabang</a> and the Plain of Jars, and fell back only after outrunning their supply lines. In May 1953 Premier René Mayer named Henri Navarre to command <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-union">French Union</a> forces, with a single order: to create military conditions that would lead to an "honorable political solution".</p><p>Navarre looked for a way to meet the threat to Laos. Colonel Louis Berteil formulated the hérisson ('hedgehog') concept, a fortified airhead set up by airlift beside key Viet Minh supply lines to Laos, which would cut off the Viet Minh fighting there and force them to withdraw. It rested on the Battle of Nà Sản in late November and early December 1952, where the French had beaten back Giáp's forces repeatedly, inflicting very heavy losses. The French hoped to lure Giáp into a massed assault that their artillery, armour and air support could then decimate.</p><p>In June 1953 Major General René Cogny proposed Điện Biên Phủ, which had an old airstrip built by the Japanese, as a "mooring point". Cogny imagined a lightly defended base for raids, while Navarre believed he meant a heavily fortified one able to withstand a siege. Every major subordinate officer protested, and Cogny warned of "a new Nà Sản under worse conditions". Navarre rejected the criticism, called the hedgehog "a mediocre solution" and set the operation for 20 November 1953.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Dien Bien Phu&#8221; cites where it covers Military situation, Nà Sản and the hedgehog concept, Lead up to Castor, Establishment of air operations:</p><ul><li>Phillip Davidson, Vietnam at War: The History, 1946–1975 (Oxford University Press, 1988)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Operation Castor began at 10:35 on 20 November 1953, when the French dropped or flew 9,000 troops into the valley over three days, together with a bulldozer to prepare the airstrip. From December, under Colonel Christian de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castries">Castries</a>, they turned the camp into a fortress with seven satellite positions. The fortified headquarters stood in the centre, with Huguette to the west, Claudine to the south and Dominique to the northeast. The other positions were Anne-Marie to the northwest, Beatrice to the northeast, Gabrielle to the north and Isabelle, 6 km to the south, covering the reserve airstrip.</p><p>The garrison numbered 10,800 troops and, with further reinforcements, nearly 16,000. It had ten US M24 Chaffee light tanks, flown in in parts, artillery and aircraft, and included regular French troops, paratroopers, Foreign Legionnaires, Algerian and Moroccan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirailleurs">tirailleurs</a> and locally recruited Indochinese infantry. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> moved up to 50,000 regular troops into the surrounding hills, five divisions including the 351st Heavy Division, an artillery formation. Their artillery and anti-aircraft guns outnumbered the French by about four to one. Giáp compared the valley to a "<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> bowl", with his troops on the rim and the French at the bottom.</p><p>The Viet Minh had spent months stockpiling ammunition and siting guns in camouflaged casemates, and spies posing as camp labourers gave them the French artillery positions. The French garrison first came under direct artillery fire on 31 January 1954. Giáp had planned a fast assault on the Chinese "Fast Strike, Fast Victory" model, but cancelled it on 26 January and adopted "Steady Fight, Steady Advance", a siege approach.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> assault began on 13 March 1954 with a bombardment of Béatrice, held by the 3rd Battalion, 13th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-foreign-legion">Foreign Legion</a> Demi-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">Brigade</a>. A shell killed the battalion commander, Major Paul Pégot, and most of his staff, and Lieutenant Colonel Jules Gaucher, commander of the central subsector, was killed soon after. The 312th Division then attacked, and Béatrice was not entirely taken until after 01:00 on 14 March. The French artillery commander, Colonel Charles Piroth, distraught at being unable to silence the camouflaged Viet Minh batteries, killed himself with a grenade two days later. The airstrip was put out of commission on 14 March, and supplies now had to come by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parachute">parachute</a>.</p><p>The Viet Minh attacked Gabrielle on the night of 14 March, and its Algerian battalion fell back the next morning. On 17 March most of the Tai troops on Anne-Marie left or defected. A lull followed until 30 March, in which the French suffered a crisis of command, and Isabelle was effectively cut off. On 30 March the Viet Minh captured Dominique 1 and 2 and Eliane 1, and fighting for Eliane and Dominique continued for days, with French counterattacks retaking and losing positions.</p><p>On 5 April Giáp changed to entrenchment and sapping. The French retook Eliane 1 on 10 and 11 April, but the Viet Minh overran Huguette 1 on 22 April and took more than 90 percent of the airfield. On 1 May they overran Eliane 1, Dominique 3 and Huguette 5. On 6 May they used Katyusha rockets and detonated a mine under Eliane 2. On 7 May Giáp ordered an all-out attack with over 25,000 men, and the last position, Lily, surrendered that night.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Dien Bien Phu&#8221; cites where it covers Béatrice, Gabrielle, Anne-Marie, Lull:</p><ul><li>Phillip Davidson, Vietnam at War: The History, 1946–1975 (Oxford University Press, 1988)</li><li>Kevin Boylan, Valley of the Shadow: The Siege of Dien Bien Phu (Osprey Press, 2018)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pierre-mendes-france"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pierre-mendes-france.jpg" alt="Pierre Mendès France" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pierre-mendes-france">Pierre Mendès France</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mend%C3%A8s-France_Harcourt_1948.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Studio Harcourt, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>On 8 May the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> counted 11,721 prisoners, of whom 4,436 were wounded, about one third of all those they had captured in the war. Able-bodied prisoners were force-marched more than 600 km to camps to the north and east, and hundreds died of disease on the way. The Red Cross extracted 858 of the wounded. Only around 200 French soldiers escaped, towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luang-prabang">Luang Prabang</a>. Of 10,863 prisoners, including Vietnamese fighting for the French, 3,290 were repatriated four months later, though the figure may include 3,013 prisoners of Vietnamese origin whose fate is unknown.</p><p>French casualties recorded until 5 May were 1,142 killed in action, 429 who died of wounds, 1,606 missing, 4,436 wounded and 1,161 deserters, not counting many more killed and wounded on 5, 6 and 7 May. Another source gives about 2,293 killed and 5,134 wounded. The Vietnamese government reported 4,020 dead, 9,118 wounded and 792 missing; the French estimated 8,000 dead and 15,000 wounded. Mark Moyar lists 22,900 out of 50,000.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> Conference opened on 8 May, the day after the surrender. The July agreement partitioned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> into a communist North and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-vietnam">State of Vietnam</a>, which opposed it. The elections meant to reunite the zones in 1956 were never held, and the last French forces left in 1956. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> the news caused shock, and Prime Minister Joseph Laniel resigned in June, replaced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-mendes-france">Pierre Mendès France</a>. In mid June the French ordered isolated positions abandoned, and Groupement Mobile 100 was ambushed at Mang Yang Pass.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Dien Bien Phu&#8221; cites where it covers Aftermath, Disaster in the Central Highlands, Prisoners, Casualties:</p><ul><li>Martin Windrow, Dien Bien Phu 1954: The French Defeat that Lured America Into Vietnam (Osprey, 2021)</li><li>Anthony Tucker-Jones, Dien Bien Phu : the first Indochina War, 1946-1954 (Barnsley, South Yorkshire : Pen &amp; Sword Military, 2017)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The surviving accounts are mostly by participants and later historians. Henri Navarre wrote of how <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> commanders, under the influence of Chinese advisers, had dug in their artillery by single pieces and fired point-blank, which "was to make shambles of all the estimates" of French artillerymen. Phillip Davidson, a military scholar, described the state of French command that Navarre found on arrival. Bernard Fall, Martin Windrow and Jules Roy are cited on the events inside the camp. A commission under General Georges Catroux reported on the conduct of operations under Navarre.</p><p>Several points are disputed. Fall, using Colonel Pierre Langlais's memoirs, records that on 24 March Langlais and fellow paratroop commanders confronted de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castries">Castries</a> in his bunker and took effective command. Davidson thought Langlais did take over effective command, while Roy does not mention the event and Windrow argues it is unlikely to have happened. Historians also disagree about the nickname "Angel of Điện Biên Phủ" for the flight nurse Geneviève de Galard: Windrow holds that the garrison used only her name, and Michael Kenney and Fall that press agencies added the nickname.</p><p>The casualty figures rest on different counts, and French and Vietnamese totals differ widely. Max Hastings stated that in 2018 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a> had still not credibly enumerated its losses. The French estimates of Viet Minh losses at Béatrice were 600 dead and 1,200 wounded, and the Viet Minh stated 193 killed and 137 wounded. The role of CIA controlled airlines was little known until Robbins published it after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a> and was not officially acknowledged until the 21st century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ferhat-abbas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ferhat-abbas.jpg" alt="Ferhat Abbas" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ferhat-abbas">Ferhat Abbas</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ferhat_Abbas_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Anefo, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Điện Biên Phủ was the decisive battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-indochina-war">First Indochina War</a>, and the loss of the garrison meant that roughly one tenth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-union">French Union</a> manpower in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">Indochina</a> had been lost. The defeat did not significantly alter the strategic situation, but brought a profound psychological shock to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The Catroux commission concluded that the fall was, in strictly military terms, a very serious failure that did not upset the balance of forces, and took on the aspect of definitive defeat through its effect on a French public tired of an unpopular war.</p><p>Outside Indochina the political significance was far-reaching. The Algerian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-algeria">National Liberation Front</a> viewed it as an epoch-changing moment, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferhat-abbas">Ferhat Abbas</a>, post-colonial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>'s first president, called it "the 'Valmy' of the colonised peoples". Colonel William F. Long wrote twelve years later that Dien Bien Phu had become shorthand for a defeat of the West by the East. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, which was underwriting 80% of French expenditure in Indochina, had avoided overt intervention, though it covertly supplied pilots and aircraft.</p><p>In 1968 the North Vietnamese Army under Giáp besieged the US base at Khe Sanh, but there were differences: the Marines held the high ground, the supply base was 45 km away and Khe Sanh received 18,000 tons of air resupply in 77 days against 4,000 tons in 167 days at Điện Biên Phủ. Today the site is a popular tourist attraction with a modern museum, preserved French positions and memorials. The battle is the subject of Pierre Schoendoerffer's 1992 film Dien Bien Phu.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>From 13 March to 7 May 1954 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> under Giáp besieged and overran the French camp at Điện Biên Phủ. The defeat shocked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> Accords then partitioned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> temporarily at the 17th parallel.</em></p>

